# Diffusion of Innovation

Rogers' model of how an innovation spreads through a population over time, producing the S-curve. Adopter categories: **innovators (~2.5%) · early adopters (~13.5%) · early majority (~34%) · late majority (~34%) · laggards (~16%)**.

## The five constructs (as taught)
Innovations succeed when they are broadly adopted. What decides that:

| Construct | Effect on adoption | Question it answers |
|---|---|---|
| **Relative advantage** | Increase | What problem does it solve? What is the added value — need, value, financial return? |
| **Compatibility** | Increase | How does it fit existing values, past experiences and the needs of potential adopters? |
| **Complexity** | **Lower** | How hard is it to understand and use? |
| **Trialability** | Increase | Can it be experimented with first? |
| **Observability** | Increase | Are the results visible to others? |

## Use in this course
Two jobs. First, the test for whether something is still a [[Trend]]: past the early majority it is becoming the norm. Second, week 1 asks explicitly **how you would divide your industry using Rogers' diffusion** — where do the firms in it sit on the curve?

The running case is the **Dutch self-checkout, launched 2005**: who considers themselves an early adopter, who has used a smart cart, who has shopped at a robotic convenience store.

